-- Per-machine monitor layout. hyprland.lua does an optional
-- `pcall(require, "monitors")`, so with no monitors.lua present it falls back
-- to the `output = ""` catch-all (preferred mode, auto position, AUTO SCALE)
-- and everything still works -- just not necessarily at the scale you want.
--
-- The easy way to create one is not to edit this file at all. Arrange your
-- displays however you like, confirm it looks right, then freeze it:
--
--     ~/HyKr/Scripts/snapshot_monitors.sh
--
-- That reads the live layout out of `hyprctl monitors` and writes
-- ~/.config/hypr/monitors.lua with the real output names, modes, positions,
-- scales and rotations for THIS machine. Re-run it after rearranging
-- displays. `--print` shows what it would write without writing it.
--
-- Copy this file to `monitors.lua` beside it only if you would rather write it
-- by hand. Either way it is gitignored, so it survives `git pull` and can
-- never be committed onto someone else's outputs.
--
-- Why it is not in hyprland.lua any more: output names are not distinctive.
-- HDMI-A-1 is the first HDMI output and eDP-1 the internal panel on nearly any
-- machine, so a layout committed to the tracked config meant anyone installing
-- HyKr whose output happened to match got the author's resolution, position
-- and rotation applied on first login, before they had a terminal open to undo
-- it. The catch-all never helped: the explicit rules match first.
--
-- Two things worth pinning even when the rest is fine, because NEITHER
-- survives a config reload on its own -- set one live with `hyprctl keyword`
-- and it is never written back, so the next `hyprctl reload` drops it:
--
--   scale      the catch-all's `scale = "auto"` picks a DPI-derived, often
--              fractional scale. If your scaling ever changes for no visible
--              reason, this is why, and `scale = 1` here is the fix.
--   transform  0 = normal, 1 = 90 deg, 2 = 180 deg, 3 = 270 deg
--
-- Get the real values for this machine from:  hyprctl monitors

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- Examples

-- A single laptop panel -- the common case. `preferred` lets the panel report
-- its own native resolution and refresh rate rather than hardcoding a guess,
-- while scale stays pinned so it cannot drift to something automatic.
-- hl.monitor({ output = "eDP-1", mode = "preferred", position = "0x0", scale = 1 })

-- The same panel with everything pinned explicitly (1080p, 144 Hz):
-- hl.monitor({ output = "eDP-1", mode = "1920x1080@144", position = "0x0", scale = 1 })

-- A three-monitor desktop, two of them rotated. Positions are the top-left
-- corner of each output in the combined layout, in post-rotation pixels --
-- which is why the 1080-wide gap in front of DP-2 and DP-3 is the height of
-- the 90-degree-rotated HDMI-A-1 beside them.
-- hl.monitor({ output = "HDMI-A-1", mode = "1920x1080@144",    position = "0x370",     scale = 1, transform = 1 }) -- 90 deg
-- hl.monitor({ output = "DP-2",     mode = "2560x1440@239.96", position = "1080x0",    scale = 1, transform = 2 }) -- 180 deg
-- hl.monitor({ output = "DP-3",     mode = "1920x1080@239.96", position = "1080x1440", scale = 1 })

-- Anything this file does not name falls through to hyprland.lua's catch-all,
-- so an occasionally-plugged-in external monitor needs no entry -- unless you
-- want a fixed scale or position for it, which is exactly when to add one.
